---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
---
<Site
  title="About · Ouijit"
  description="Ouijit is built by one person, Prentice Bjerkeseth. Free, open source under AGPL, no account and no telemetry, and the plan is to keep it that way.">
  <main class="compare-content about-content">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>Ouijit is a desktop app for running coding agents in parallel. It aims to be focused, made with care, private, and independent.</p>
    <p>
      I'm <a href="https://github.com/pbjer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prentice Bjerkeseth</a> and I make
      Ouijit alone. I also use it for all of my own work, including on Ouijit itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Focused</h2>
    <p>
      Running several agents at once means more code arrives than you can comfortably read, and the work shifts
      to keeping track of it. A tool for this should protect your focus and your judgement as that volume goes
      up, rather than ask for more of both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ouijit is for experienced engineers who carry a lot of scope: senior and staff engineers who can direct
      many pieces of work at once and want to actually read what comes back. It is deliberately not a
      tmux-style grid of panes, and it is not an editor. If either of those is what you want, Ouijit will
      probably frustrate you.
    </p>

    <h2>Made with care</h2>
    <p>
      A lot of the work in Ouijit goes into details a user might never consciously notice. Terminals sit in a
      stack instead of a busy tiled view. Each project carries its own avatar so you can tell at a glance where
      you are. The git plumbing of branches and worktrees is handled for you, so you think about the task and
      not the checkout. Interactions are deliberately tactile, closer to handling objects than operating a
      dashboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      The agents themselves run as the CLIs they already are, in real terminals. Their capabilities and tooling
      move fastest at the command line, and I don't want Ouijit to lag behind them by wrapping them. A chat
      interface is planned, but it will sit on top of the terminal, not replace it.
    </p>

    <h2>Private</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account and no login. There is no telemetry. I don't think a tool like this should require
      either, and I don't think you should have to give up privacy to get something well made.
    </p>

    <h2>Independent</h2>
    <p>
      Ouijit is free. If it ever charges for something, it will be a feature with an inherent running cost
      beyond my time, such as hosted environments or sync. The app itself will stay free.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is open source under AGPL-3.0. I wanted it to be open, and I didn't want a company to be able to take
      the work and profit from it easily.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many tools in this category are raising venture money, getting acquired, or shutting down. I intend for
      this one to still be standing, still free, and still private, and to build something special as an
      individual.
    </p>
  </main>
</Site>
